  <p>If a page on another site offers your product without your permission, you can report it from your dashboard. We check the page and prepare a takedown notice for you. You read it and sign it. Then we send it to the site for you. Nothing goes out until you sign.</p>
  <p>This article is not legal advice.</p>
  <h3 id="what-you-can-report">What you can report</h3>
  <p>You can report a page on another site that offers a copy of your product without your permission. Each report covers one page and one product. The link has to point straight to that page.</p>
  <p>This is not for pages on Gumroad. That includes a Gumroad seller's page on their own custom domain. To report a Gumroad seller, see <a href="286-how-do-i-report-a-gumroad-creator">Report a Gumroad creator</a>.</p>
  <p>We do not send a notice if the page is a licensed or other authorized use of your work, or if the person who posted it has a license from you or is related to you. We check this for every report.</p>
  <h3 id="who-can-report">Who can report</h3>
  <p>To start a report, open the <strong>Products</strong> page, open the menu on a product, and choose <strong>Report piracy</strong>. The option shows only for accounts that have access to this feature. If you do not see it, it is not turned on for your account.</p>
  <p>Only the account owner and team members with the admin role can file, read, and sign a report. The notice has your legal name in it.</p>
  <p>To report a product, all of these must be true:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your account is not suspended.</li>
    <li>You have confirmed your email, completed a payout, and reached $100 in sales.</li>
    <li>Your payout information has a legal name. The notice shows it.</li>
    <li>The product is yours, it is published, and it has no collaborator.</li>
    <li>The product has at least one successful sale.</li>
    <li>You have filed fewer than 5 reports this month.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If something is missing, the report form lists what to fix and does not let you submit.</p>
  <h3 id="what-we-check-when-you-file">What we check when you file</h3>
  <p>When you submit a report, we check the link right away:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The link works as a link.</strong> It must start with <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>, be at most 500 characters, have no username or password in it, and not contain another link.</li>
    <li><strong>The page is not on Gumroad.</strong> A page hosted on Gumroad, including a custom domain on Gumroad, goes through the terms of service process instead.</li>
    <li><strong>The page is new for this product.</strong> If you already reported the same page for the same product, we refuse the second report. That holds whatever happened to the first one, even if we did not send it. We treat links that differ only by <code>http</code> or <code>https</code>, <code>www</code>, a trailing slash, a <code>#</code> fragment, or the order of the query parts as the same page.</li>
    <li><strong>You are under the monthly limit.</strong> You can file 5 reports each calendar month. Every report you file counts, including ones we do not send. The count starts again on the first day of the month (UTC).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="what-we-check-on-the-page">What we check on the page</h3>
  <p>After you file, the report shows <strong>Checking the page</strong>. We look at the page and ask three things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Does the page offer your work?</li>
    <li>Is it some other authorized use of your work?</li>
    <li>Does the person who posted it have a license from you, or are they related to you?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If we cannot decide, a person on our team decides. We also run the account and link checks again, so a change since you filed, such as a suspended account, can stop the report.</p>
  <p>Three things can happen:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>We confirm the page.</strong> We prepare your notice and email you a link to review and sign it. The report shows <strong>Ready for you to sign</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>We cannot confirm the page.</strong> We send nothing, and the report shows <strong>Not sent</strong>. You cannot file the same page again for that product. If you have more evidence, contact support.</li>
    <li><strong>We confirm the page, but we do not have a verified takedown contact for the site.</strong> We send notices only to a contact we have checked, from the Copyright Office list of designated agents or the site's own DMCA page. Until our team adds one, the report stays on <strong>Checking the page</strong>. We do not send to an address we have not checked.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="sign-the-notice">Sign the notice</h3>
  <p>Open the report from the email, or from the <strong>Piracy reports</strong> tab on your Products page. You see the full notice before you sign. It is written to the site's copyright agent (the person the site names to receive these notices) and has:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your legal name, as the owner. It says Gumroad sends the notice for you, with your written permission.</li>
    <li>Your product and its link, and the link to the page that offers it.</li>
    <li>Your contact details: your name, your email, and Gumroad's address.</li>
    <li>A statement that you believe in good faith that the page is not authorized by you, your agent, or the law.</li>
    <li>A statement that the information in the notice is accurate, and a statement, under penalty of perjury, that you own the work or can act for the owner.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To sign, check each of these five statements:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The notice shows your legal name and email address. The site can share the notice with the person who posted the page, and the notice may be published, for example on Lumen, a public database of takedown notices.</li>
    <li>You have read the notice, and you make its statements yourself, including the one under penalty of perjury.</li>
    <li>If you knowingly say a page infringes your work when it does not, you can be liable for damages, costs, and attorneys' fees under U.S. copyright law (17 U.S.C. § 512(f)).</li>
    <li>You thought about whether the page could be a licensed use or a fair use of your work, such as a review, a commentary, or a short excerpt.</li>
    <li>You authorize Gumroad to send the notice by email for you, and to put your typed name on it as your signature.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Then type your full legal name and click <strong>Sign notice</strong>. We add <code>Signed: /s/</code>, your name, and the date to the end of the notice. After that, the text cannot change. If we change the wording of the statements while your page is open, we ask you to reload and read them again.</p>
  <p>We do not set a deadline to sign. The report waits until you do.</p>
  <h3 id="after-we-send-the-notice">After we send the notice</h3>
  <p>After you sign, the report shows <strong>Signed, sending soon</strong>. We check for signed notices every 15 minutes. Then we email the exact text you signed from support@gumroad.com to the site's takedown contact, with you in CC. We email you when it is sent. The report then shows <strong>Sent, waiting for the site</strong>.</p>
  <p>Before a notice goes out, we check that the text is the one you signed and that the site's contact has not changed since we checked the page. If either has changed, the notice waits and our team looks at it.</p>
  <p>The report history shows <strong>The site received the notice</strong> when our email service confirms delivery. If the email bounces, or we get no delivery confirmation after 24 hours, our team is alerted and checks the site's contact. We do not send the notice again by ourselves. If the contact has changed, our team can tell you through support.</p>
  <p>Replies from the site go to a Gumroad address that belongs to your report, so we can match each one to it.</p>
  <h3 id="if-the-site-gets-a-counter-notice">If the site gets a counter-notice</h3>
  <p>The person who posted the page can answer your notice with a counter-notice. It says they have the right to share the work. They send it to the site, and it can reach us by reply to your notice.</p>
  <p>When we record a counter-notice, the report shows <strong>Disputed</strong>, and we email you the full text right away. Under U.S. copyright law (17 U.S.C. § 512(g)), the site can put the page back <strong>10 to 14 business days</strong> after it received the counter-notice. The report shows the dates this covers. They count weekdays only and do not allow for holidays, so treat them as approximate.</p>
  <p>To stop the page from coming back, file a court action against the person who posted it before the first date in that window, and reply to our email to tell us. We do not file for you. If you do nothing, the site can put the page back.</p>
  <p>A counter-notice can also arrive after the site has already removed the page, or has not answered. In that case the report opens again and shows <strong>Disputed</strong>.</p>
  <h3 id="how-a-report-ends">How a report ends</h3>
  <p>When a report ends, we record the result and email you. The report shows one of these:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Page removed.</strong> The site took the page down.</li>
    <li><strong>No response from the site.</strong> The site did not answer your notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Page put back.</strong> The site put the page back after a counter-notice.</li>
    <li><strong>Withdrawn.</strong> The notice was withdrawn. We record a reason.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you close your account, we close any report whose notice was not sent yet, and it shows <strong>Closed</strong>. We keep the record. Reports that were sent stay on file.</p>
  <h3 id="timelines">Timelines</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Checking the page:</strong> usually less than a day. If a person on our team needs to look, it can take a few days. If we have no verified contact for the site, we cannot say when it will move.</li>
    <li><strong>Signing:</strong> no deadline.</li>
    <li><strong>Sending:</strong> we look for signed notices every 15 minutes. A notice can wait, for example if one of our checks fails, until our team looks at it.</li>
    <li><strong>The site's answer:</strong> no set time. We do not close a report on a timer, so a report can stay on <strong>Sent, waiting for the site</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>A counter-notice:</strong> we email you the full text right away. The site can put the page back 10 to 14 business days after it received the counter-notice, so act before the first of those days.</li>
    <li><strong>Monthly limit:</strong> 5 reports each calendar month, starting again on the first day (UTC).</li>
  </ul>
